<!--{ bek4 : markdown }-->
# Beam check

A simply supported steel beam under a uniform load. The section properties
come from the library [steel.texl](library/steel.texl).
<% texl.import("library/steel.texl") ~> (lib -> bind "steel" lib.data) %>
<!--{ lod2 : markdown }-->
## Input

<%
L = 6,                       # span in m
gk = 4.0,                    # permanent load in kN/m
qk = 8.5,                    # variable load in kN/m
section = steel.IPE.IPE300
%>
- Span: <% L %> m
- Section: <% section.name %>, steel <% steel.grade.name %>
- Loads: g<sub>k</sub> = <% gk %> kN/m, q<sub>k</sub> = <% qk %> kN/m
<!--[ Anna: span and loads from the client's brief, rev. B. ]-->
<!--{ saf7 : markdown }-->
## Design load

<%
gs = section.mass * 9.81 / 1000,   # self-weight in kN/m
qd = 1.35 * (gk + gs) + 1.5 * qk,
MEd = qd * L ^ 2 / 8
%>
The self-weight is $g_s = <% math.round (gs * 100) / 100 %>\ \text{kN/m}$, so
the design load and the midspan moment are:

$$
q_d = 1.35\,(g_k + g_s) + 1.5\,q_k = <% math.round (qd * 100) / 100 %>\ \text{kN/m}
$$

$$
M_{Ed} = \frac{q_d L^2}{8} = <% math.round (MEd * 10) / 10 %>\ \text{kNm}
$$

<!--[ Marco: partial factors from EN 1990, table A1.2(B). ]-->
<!--{ siz5 : markdown }-->
## Bending check

<%
sigma = MEd * 1000000 / section.W,
fyd = steel.grade.fy / steel.gammaM0,
u = sigma / fyd
%>
<!--{ buf9 : table }-->
| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Bending stress $\sigma = M_{Ed} / W$: | <% math.round sigma %> N/mm² |
| Design strength $f_{yd} = f_y / \gamma_{M0}$: | <% math.round fyd %> N/mm² |
| Utilisation $u = \sigma / f_{yd}$: | <% math.round (u * 100) %> % |
<!--{ bab5 : markdown }-->

The <% section.name %> is <% u <= 1 ? "**adequate**" ; "**not adequate**" %>.
<!--[ Marco: fine for the report. ]-->
<!--[ Anna: try an IPE 270 too. ]-->
<!--{ cos3 : markdown }-->
## Cost

<%
price = 2,                   # steel price per kg
cost = price * section.mass * L
%>
Steel sells at $<% price %> to $3 per kg: these dollars are amounts of money,
not an equation. The beam costs $C = p \, m \, L$, which is about
$<% math.round cost %>. Where a price would look like an equation, write the
dollars as `\$`: \$2/kg, or 84\$/m.
